{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775255446-Xv5gogFEJC6zJaiIeuWA","role":"assistant","created":1775255467,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Based on my research so far, I know:\n\nFrom Treasury Bulletin data:\n- January 31, 1964: Public marketable interest-bearing securities = $209,344 million\n- January 31, 1965: Public marketable interest-bearing securities = $215,070 million  \n- January 31, 1966: Public marketable interest-bearing securities = $218,069 million\n\nFor exchange rates - during 1964-1966, India maintained a fixed exchange rate regime. According to my Wikipedia research, India had a par value exchange rate until 1971, with a fixed rate of $1 to INR 7.50 starting in 1966 (after devaluation).\n\nBefore 1966, the rupee was pegged to sterling at around £1 = INR 13.33 (from the Bretton Woods system). After the devaluation in 1966, it moved to $1 = INR 7.50.\n\nBut I need to find more specific annual average USD/INR rates for 1964 and 1965. Let me think about this differently.\n\nBefore 1966, India was on a par value exchange rate regime with the pound sterling. The pound was pegged to the USD at $1 = £0.3571 (or £1 = $2.80) from 1949 until 1967.\n\nSo if we calculate:\n- £1 = INR 13.33 (approximately, the pegged rate with sterling)\n- $1 = £0.3571 (the $ to £ conversion)\n- Therefore, $1 = INR 13.33 × 0.3571 = INR 4.76 (approximately)\n\nBut that's not quite right because I need annual average rates for 1964 and 1965. Let me search more specifically for this.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_4b9ad46e3185513b","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"curl -sL \"https://fraser.stlouisfed.org/title/annual-report-federal-reserve-board-453/1965?section=4825\" 2>/dev/null | grep -E \"dollar|exchange|INR|rupee|India|1964|1965\" | head -50"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
